    "think": "Leading diagnosis: solitary fibrous tumor (SFT) of the frontal lobe. Rationale: large frontal interhemispheric mass with solid enhancing component and multicystic cysts; histology shows spindle-cell fascicles; CD34 and Bcl-2 are strongly expressed in most cells — a classic SFT immunoprofile. The presence of about half of cells with nuclear p53 can reflect heterogeneity or secondary mutation and does not exclude SFT.\n\nKey alternatives that could explain a CD34/Bcl-2–positive spindle-cell tumor:\n- GIST (gastric/intestinal-type) — CD117/c-KIT and DOG1 positive; CD34 and Bcl-2 may be positive but CD117/c-KIT is usually present.\n- Spindle-cell meningioma — may show CD34 and Bcl-2 positivity in some tumors; typically shows EMA and other meningioma markers.\n- Nodular fasciitis / other reactive spindle-cell lesions — often show CD34 and Bcl-2 but usually lack aggressive growth and enhancement pattern.\n- Primary CNS sarcoma (e.g., synovial sarcoma, undifferentiated sarcoma) — immunophenotype and molecular markers differ.\n\nWhat would most change management/diagnosis now: a literature search to confirm whether the combination of CD34+/Bcl-2+ spindle-cell lesion with prominent solid enhancement and multicystic components in the frontal lobe is diagnostic of SFT, and to identify any rare mimics with similar immunophenotype. A definitive molecular test (NAB2–STAT3 fusion by RT-PCR or FISH) would be ideal but molecular genetics results were not available; therefore a phenotype-based literature search is the highest-yield next step to resolve the differential and guide further targeted testing.\n\nTherefore the single next action is a targeted literature search on the phenotype (spindle-cell frontal brain tumor with CD34 and Bcl-2 expression and multicystic solid lesion).",
