    "payload": "Solitary fibrous tumor (solitary fibrous tumor of the brain)",
    "think": "Leading diagnosis: solitary fibrous tumor (SFT) of the brain (frontal interhemispheric lesion).\n\nRationale:\n- Large, well-circumscribed frontal interhemispheric mass on MRI with solid enhancing component and multicystic components — fits SFT (can be large and cystic).\n- Postoperative histopathology: spindle-cell fascicles on H&E — classic for SFT.\n- CD34 positivity and Bcl-2 positivity — both commonly expressed in SFT (CD34 supports SFT; Bcl-2 supports SFT).\n- Nuclear p53 expression in about half of cells — can be seen in SFTs and is not diagnostic but does not exclude SFT.\n- Key alternatives: meningioma (can be CD34+ but usually EMA/PR positive and different histology), solitary fibrous tumor mimics (e.g., inflammatory pseudotumor), and primary CNS lymphoma (usually B-cell markers, EBV+, different morphology). These are less likely given the histology and IHC.\n- Safety concerns / missing data: molecular confirmation (STAT6 nuclear positivity and NAB2–STAT3 fusion) would be ideal, but molecular testing was not available in the record. The current evidence (size, MRI features, histology, CD34/Bcl-2) is sufficient for a working diagnosis.\n\nGiven the available imaging and histopathology/IHC, the single best action now is to make the most likely diagnosis so management (surgery planning, adjuvant therapy, surveillance) can proceed."
